  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1106 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nLate evening Water Vapor satellite trends showed a cutoff upper-\nlevel low centered over southwest Arizona with the remnants of\nHurricane Polo now located over northwest Mexico. An upper-level\nridge over portions of the Southern Plains and Lower Mississippi\nValley and a surface high centered over the Mid-Atlantic states\nis still bringing dry weather to the Mid-South. Late evening\ntemperatures were mostly in the 60s to lower 70s with surface\ndewpoints recovering back into the 50s.\n\nSoutherly winds on the back side of high pressure will gradually\nbring a slow return of moisture to the Mid-South into Thursday\nwith minimum relative humidity values not as low as the past few\ndays. Most locations will see a return of 90 degree temperatures\nWednesday and especially on Thursday. Confidence remains high for\ndry weather to persist into Thursday.\n\nDeterministic and ensemble model solutions are in agreement with\nthe upper-level low lifting into portions of the Central and\nSouthern Plains, then transitioning into an open wave trough as\nit moves into the Middle and Lower Mississippi Valleys. Rain\nchances will gradually begin to develop late Thursday night into\nFriday morning as the right entrance of an upper-level jet streak\ndevelops across the region and a weak cold front slowly moves\nacross the Lower Mississippi and Tennessee Valleys. \n\nPrecipitable water values are anticipated to approach the 99th \npercentile around 2 inches this weekend. Forecast rainfall amounts\nhave dropped again in this latest forecast cycle with amounts \naveraging near 1 inch towards the MO/KY border to 1.5 inches or \ngreater along and south of I-40. Severe weather is not anticipated\nat this time with marginal instability and weak overall shear \nexpected. Cooler and drier air will filter in behind this front \nfor early next week with highs in the 70s to around 80 degrees \nwith lows mostly in the 50s.",
